<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Recovery principles"><div><strong>Rehydrate</strong><span>Replace meaningful fluid and sodium losses when the session produced substantial sweating.</span></div><div><strong>Refuel and repair</strong><span>Carbohydrate and protein needs are not covered by hydration alone.</span></div><div><strong>Rest matters</strong><span>Sleep and recovery time remain central to adaptation and readiness for the next session.</span></div></div>

<section class="bhd-page__feature"><div><p class="bhd-page__kicker">The recovery window</p><h2>The next session determines how aggressive recovery needs to be</h2><p>An athlete who has another hard session later the same day has different priorities from someone with a full rest day ahead. Rapid recovery can make structured fluid, sodium and carbohydrate replacement more useful, while a normal meal and regular drinking may be enough after a shorter or easier session.</p><p>Recent recovery frameworks describe rehydration alongside refuelling, repair and rest. That is a useful guardrail because no beverage can do all four jobs by itself.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__split"><div class="bhd-page__split-media"><img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/4148942/pexels-photo-4148942.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1500" alt="Runner drinking water during a training break in a park" width="1500" height="1000" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div><div class="bhd-page__split-copy"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Rehydration</p><h2>Replace fluid with enough sodium to retain it when losses were large</h2><p>After heavy sweating, plain water can restore fluid but may be less effective for rapid fluid retention when sodium losses are substantial. Sodium-containing beverages and food can support restoration of fluid balance.</p><p>Exact replacement targets depend on how much fluid was lost, how quickly recovery is needed, what food is being eaten and the athlete's health. A single fixed percentage should not be treated as mandatory for every workout.</p></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">The full recovery system</p><h2>Four jobs after demanding exercise</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4"><article><h3>Rehydrate</h3><p>Restore fluid and electrolytes according to losses and the time before the next session.</p></article><article><h3>Refuel</h3><p>Use adequate carbohydrate from food and drinks when glycogen restoration matters.</p></article><article><h3>Repair</h3><p>Include high-quality protein as part of recovery nutrition when muscle repair and adaptation are priorities.</p></article><article><h3>Rest</h3><p>Sleep, reduced load and time between sessions are not replaceable by supplements or beverages.</p></article></div></section>

<section class="bhd-page__prose-grid"><article class="bhd-page__prose"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Where Best Hydrate may fit</p><h2>Use it for the hydration part of recovery</h2><p>Best Hydrate can contribute fluid, glucose and listed electrolytes after exercise when prepared according to the current label.</p><p>It is not a complete recovery meal. The current product is intentionally positioned as a hydration mix, so athletes with substantial post-exercise carbohydrate or protein needs should obtain those from the rest of their recovery nutrition.</p></article><aside class="bhd-page__callout"><span class="bhd-page__callout-label">Simple rule</span><h3>Hydrate with the drink, recover with the whole routine.</h3><p>Pair hydration with food, rest and the athlete's next-session demands.</p></aside></section>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section"><p class="bhd-page__kicker">Use context</p><h2>Recovery changes with what came before</h2><div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--3"><article><h3>Endurance</h3><p>Long events can create larger fluid and fuel deficits.</p><a href="/pages/endurance">Endurance →</a></article><article><h3>Heat & High Sweat</h3><p>Heavy sweat loss increases rehydration priority.</p><a href="/pages/heat-high-sweat">Heat & High Sweat →</a></article><article><h3>Everyday activity</h3><p>Not every workout needs specialized recovery nutrition.</p><a href="/pages/active-living">Active Living →</a></article></div></section>

